  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Reliance Standalone vs Consolidated: Equity Bridge Reconciliation",
  "scenario": "You're an equity analyst reviewing Reliance Industries' latest annual report. The company publishes both standalone (parent only) and consolidated (whole group) statements. Your Mumbai desk has flagged a query: does the consolidated equity bridge reconcile correctly through profit, dividends, and OCI? And where does the group's debt actually sit — parent or subsidiaries?",
  "instructions": "Reconcile Reliance's consolidated equity bridge using opening equity, net profit, dividends, OCI and capital changes; verify closing equity ties out. Then compare standalone and consolidated revenue and debt to identify where the economics actually reside.",
  "learning_objective": "Verify inter-statement integrity by reconciling the equity bridge across profit, dividends and OCI; and identify structural differences between standalone and consolidated statements to locate where revenue, debt and cash reside in a conglomerate.",
  "hints": [
    "The equity bridge formula is: Opening Equity + Net Profit − Dividends + OCI ± Capital Changes = Closing Equity. Check if your calculated closing equity matches the balance sheet's actual closing figure.",
    "Consolidated revenue and profit are much larger than standalone because subsidiaries' full results are included; consolidated debt may sit in subsidiaries to keep the parent's balance sheet clean — compare the two to see which entity is the true operating business.",
    "For the equity reconciliation, ensure you pull opening equity from the prior year's balance sheet (closing ₹47,500 Cr becomes this year's opening ₹47,500 Cr), add this year's profit and OCI from the P&L, subtract dividends from the notes, and verify the sum equals the balance sheet's closing equity (₹52,890 Cr)."
  ],
